Subject: Wellma – Summary of Discussion with L Catterton
Hi all,
Please find below a summary of our discussion with L Catterton on the Wellma process. They remain constructive overall, with a number of specific diligence questions.
Overall View L Catterton is positive on the growth story and views the plan as reasonably conservative. They are engaged and aligned on several areas of focus.
What They Like
	•	Strong top-line growth despite some churn (a mix of deliberate and unexplained).
	•	Management’s projections appear conservative — only ~4–5 percentage points above base-level growth.
	•	Comfortable with the US expansion approach so far (targeted rather than a broad distraction).
	•	The business has continued to grow through the issues observed.
Key Questions & Areas for Further Diligence
Customer resilience & dual sourcing They would like better colour on how the dual-sourcing dynamic has played out and what mitigation has been put in place. Some customers have stopped certain formats or reduced volume, which gives them a degree of pause, even though overall growth has remained strong.
Format mix They want to understand the rationale for entering certain lower-margin tech carrier / commodity-type contracts. Specifically, whether the new sizable contracts are justified by cash generation, capacity utilisation, or future multi-format expansion opportunities with those clients.
Customer mix (insurgents vs larger customers) The current book remains heavily skewed toward insurgents (~70%+). The pipeline is 21 of 32 insurgent (slightly lower concentration). They would like more insight into the thinking behind the push toward larger, more scaled home-care type customers (not full CPG).
Customer acquisition model The model is currently largely inbound-driven. They asked whether this can be accelerated with a more proactive, front-foot sales approach, and what role site managers play in the current model.
Integration & cross-sell Given many customers appear to select the business for proximity, they questioned how significant the real cross-sell opportunity is. They also noted that current site-level incentives (focused on maximising that site’s sales) may limit cross-selling.
Cost saves The plan shows >€8bn of cost saves. Government-related saves appear well below prior expectations (high-teens % of costs). They asked whether there is more to go after.
Capacity, CapEx & operational leverage CapEx looks relatively modest relative to the volume growth. They would like to understand how much of the margin improvement is true operational leverage versus further investment required to support the growth.
US Expansion They were initially cautious that a full US push could be a distraction. The current targeted approach in a relatively differentiated space feels sensible. Larger deals could accelerate the build-out if done at sensible multiples and properly resourced. They emphasised the importance of not diverting management focus from the still-substantial European opportunity.
Process & Valuation Views
	•	Several parties remain active and are spending money (including through ICs).
	•	CVC has dropped — they viewed competition as still strong and saw themselves as too far from the ~€1bn number (even aiming a bit below was not attractive enough).
	•	They do not expect buyers to reach €1bn (>15x on ~€60m).
	•	Sentiment is disciplined. Their view of where you need to be to stay well-placed for the next round is roughly €850–900m (14–15x on a lower number).
	•	There is an open question on whether buyers will underwrite on the full ~€60m or a lower figure to reflect ramp-rate risk.
	•	The process has felt reasonably open on Q&A so far. Overall expectations appear to have moderated somewhat versus the initial marketing.
They are happy to reconnect next week once both sides have progressed further with other parties / ICs.
Happy to discuss.
